      "heading": "Chains, and where the straight line breaks",
      "narration": "LangChain's central idea is composition, and it is a good idea. Everything is a runnable — a prompt template, a model, an output parser, a retriever — and runnables compose with a pipe operator. Prompt pipe model pipe parser. Read left to right, data flows through, and the whole thing has one interface so you can swap any piece. For a linear pipeline it is genuinely pleasant, and it removes a real amount of boilerplate around formatting, invoking, streaming and parsing. This design was a good fit for its moment, and the reason is worth noticing. Almost every early LLM application was linear. Take the user's question. Retrieve some documents. Stuff them into a prompt. Call the model. Parse the output. Return it. That is a straight line with five stations on it, and a chain is exactly the right abstraction for a straight line. Nobody was wrong to build it. Then applications got more ambitious, and the requirements that arrive are exactly the ones a straight line cannot express. If the first retrieval finds nothing useful, try a different query strategy — that is a retry with state. Classify the request first, and send it down one of three different paths — that is a branch. Generate an answer, check it against the sources, and if the check fails, generate again with the criticism included — that is a loop, and a line has no loops. Pause here, wait for a human to approve the action, and resume tomorrow from where you stopped — that is durable state, and a function call has none. You can express all of these in a chain, with conditional runnables and callbacks and branch objects, and people did. What you end up with is a control-flow graph written sideways in a composition syntax that was designed for pipelines. It works and it is hard to read, hard to test, and hard to reason about, because the actual structure of the computation is scattered across nested runnables instead of being written down anywhere. That experience — a whole community discovering that their applications were graphs and their tool was a pipe — is the direct reason the next slide exists. LangGraph is not a competitor to LangChain; it is the same team saying that the primitive was wrong once applications stopped being linear, and that the honest thing to do is make the graph explicit.",
