      "heading": "LangGraph: state, nodes, edges, cycles",
      "narration": "LangGraph's model is a state machine, and if that phrase sounds unfashionable it is because state machines are old, well understood and correct for this problem. Three concepts and you have it. First, state: you declare a typed object, usually a dictionary with a schema, that represents everything the run knows. Messages so far, retrieved documents, the current draft, a retry counter, whatever your application needs. Every step reads it and returns updates to it. Second, nodes: a node is a function that takes the state and returns a partial update. One node retrieves. One node calls the model. One node validates. They are ordinary functions and you can unit test them, which chains made awkward. Third, edges: an edge says which node runs after which. And the important variant is the conditional edge — a function that looks at the current state and returns the name of the next node. That is your branching, and notice that it is a plain Python function you can read and test, not a configuration object. Now the feature that justifies the whole redesign: cycles are first class. An edge can point backwards. The agent loop from the last lesson — call the model, run a tool, go back to the model — is two nodes and an edge returning to the first, and you write it exactly like that. Compare hiding a while-loop inside a chain abstraction that was designed for pipelines. The graph makes the loop visible in the structure, which means you can see it, draw it, and put a step counter on it in the obvious place. Then checkpointing, which is the feature teams end up depending on most and rarely anticipate. Because the state is an explicit object, LangGraph can persist it after every node. That makes a run durable, and durability unlocks a set of things that are painful otherwise. You can interrupt a run and resume it hours later. You can inspect the exact state at step four when something went wrong at step seven. You can rewind to an earlier checkpoint, change something, and replay from there. And human-in-the-loop stops being an architectural problem: pausing for approval before a destructive action is a node that interrupts, and resuming is a call with the approval in the state. In a chain, adding that means restructuring; in a graph it is an edge. The trade is verbosity, and it is a genuine trade. A LangGraph agent is more setup code than the equivalent chain — the state schema, the nodes, the edges, the compile step. What you get for it is a structure you can draw on a whiteboard and that matches the code exactly, nodes you can test individually, and a run you can stop and inspect. For a demo that is overhead. For a system that runs for months and that somebody other than you has to debug, it is the difference between maintainable and not.",
